---
// Custom Crediverso case study. Notion drives metadata; this file owns
// the body. The narrative spine: a product's identity is the tiebreaker
// when SWOTs run even. Family-first banking, in the same gesture as
// money movement, in two languages from the first sketch.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import CrediversoNavSwot from "../../components/case-study/showcase/CrediversoNavSwot.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Crediverso — Jon Tollefson"
  description="A bilingual bank where family is a feature, not a footnote. V1 of an iOS mobile banking app for the US Hispanic community."
  ogImage="/case-studies/crediverso/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 03 · February 2021 — November 2022"
    title="Crediverso"
    subtitle="A bilingual bank where family is a feature, not a footnote."
    cover="/case-studies/crediverso/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer · onboarding + credit card flows" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "Feb 2021 — Nov 2022 (14-month V1)" },
      { label: "Team", value: "Three-person design team" },
      { label: "Outcome", value: "Shipped iOS · 15% bounce drop · 125% pre-launch growth" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE PITCH ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="Crediverso was the first US company to offer credit checks in Spanish for free. The next move was an all-in-one mobile bank for the same audience. I joined the design team eight months in — three of us shipping V1 over fourteen months." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The mandate was simple to say and hard to ship: V1 of a bilingual banking product in a market that had been failed by fragmented, English-only financial tooling. The audience was US Hispanic households. The hardest decisions were never about whether to ship bilingual — they were about which product identity got to drive every screen's information hierarchy when two equally defensible options came up.</p>

      <p>This case study tells the story of the V1 nav-bar decision: the moment a SWOT ran even on the matrix and the product thesis broke the tie. It is the design move I keep coming back to.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        When SWOTs resolve evenly, the tiebreaker is rarely "best UX." It's "which option reinforces the product's identity."
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 14, label: "months, brief to App Store", color: "white" },
        { n: 3, label: "designers on the system team", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 15, label: "drop in primary-flow bounce", suffix: "%", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 125, label: "follower growth before launch", suffix: "%", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · WHO WE BUILT FOR ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="Who we built for" deck="We anchored every sprint in three customer avatars synthesized from Zoom interviews, web analytics, social listening, and competitor research. Juan was the principal. Mateo was the gap-test. Maria was the household coordinator.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/crediverso/notion-35fed8d4704f802aaba4fabba59d3f49.png"
      alt="Mateo customer avatar card. Quotes: 'I had to apply for a credit card for my mom because she doesn't speak English.' 'I'm constantly researching remittance options to send money to my family.' Profile: 20-35, Mexico/Cuba/Puerto Rico, college-educated, $50-70k. Behavior: watches Univision and Telemundo, gets news from the cheaper room on Espanola, has limited financial literacy. Help: address language barriers, create family-focused banking, eliminate friction."
      caption="Avatars rode every sprint as gut-checks. Mateo, in particular, kept the bilingual question grounded — a primary user who feels at home in English is the wrong default for this product."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Three findings drove the V1 direction:</p>
      <ul class="cv-findings">
        <li><span class="cv-pct">79%</span><span><strong>think sending money home matters.</strong> 75% already use remittance services. Remittance is a primary banking use case here, not an edge feature.</span></li>
        <li><span class="cv-pct">41M</span><span><strong>Americans are Spanish-dominant.</strong> Preference for Spanish rises sharply as the financial terminology gets more complex. Bilingual is a core requirement, not a translation pass.</span></li>
        <li><span class="cv-pct">81%</span><span><strong>asked relatives to research purchases on their behalf.</strong> Financial decisions in this market are family-coordinated, not individual.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · THREE V1 GOALS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Three V1 goals" deck="The hardest decisions of the year were the three that broke when the answers were 'both, but choose one.' We named them out loud before V1 started, and they backstopped every screen-density debate that followed.">
    <ul class="cv-goals">
      <li>
        <span class="cv-goals-num">01</span>
        <h4>One account, every product.</h4>
        <p>Users engage every partner without leaving the app. The same Figma primitives have to serve any new product without redesign.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="cv-goals-num">02</span>
        <h4>Bilingual at every step.</h4>
        <p>Spanish at every step, not a translation layer. The information hierarchy of every screen breathes for the longer language.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="cv-goals-num">03</span>
        <h4>Family as a first-class object.</h4>
        <p>Household structure becomes a product primitive. Family Cards. Funds move between cards. The product is family-first by identity.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · FAMILY AS PRIMITIVE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="Family as a primitive, not a footnote" deck="Family Cards turned household structure into a product object. Each member gets a card. Funds move between cards. The primary user sees their account and the family group at the same altitude on the home screen.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/crediverso/notion-35fed8d4704f80579fadcbd9b39006da.png"
      alt="A Garcia Family card annotated. Family balance $5,000.00 in a purple gradient surface. Avatar row of four members. Primary user: Harris. Buttons: Transactions and Spending Charts. Annotations: 'Contact photos similar to Apple OS makes for easy group visualization', 'Hidden menu to easily delete or edit family accounts', 'Key features clear and accessible.'"
      caption="Family Cards became a primitive that other partner-product surfaces inherited as the app grew. The hidden three-dot menu and the contact-photo affordances aren't decoration — they're how a Hispanic household actually reads 'this is mine, that is ours.'"
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · THE NAV-BAR SWOT ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The nav-bar SWOT" deck="Split (a dedicated Family tab) or Combined (one Accounts surface with family on the home screen). Both equally defensible. Both got real SWOTs. The matrix didn't decide. Click an option to see the home screen, the tradeoffs, and the tap-count for sending money to a cousin.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · PICK A NAV MODEL"
      caption="A V1 debate I keep coming back to. The matrix ran even — split optimized for clarity-per-context; combined optimized for fewer taps. The decision didn't resolve on the matrix. It resolved on the thesis."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <CrediversoNavSwot />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The pattern carries forward. When SWOTs resolve evenly, the tiebreaker is rarely "best UX," it's "which option reinforces the product's identity." This works on partner-product debates too: should this third-party flow open inline or in a sheet? If the matrix runs even, the question becomes which choice reads more like Crediverso. Identity becomes load-bearing.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · WHAT SHIPPED ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="What shipped" deck="V1 launched on iOS in November 2022 with full bilingual coverage across every flow. Onboarding and credit card surfaces were mine end-to-end. Family Cards, Move Money, and the Accounts home rolled out as the system-team output.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/crediverso/notion-35fed8d4704f8093b5a2ebcea6b34fb3.png"
      alt="The Add Family Members screen. Headline: Invite Family Members. Avatar list of three family members already linked: Harris (you), Maria (Mother), Jose (Father). Below: Invite new Crediverso Users form with a full-name input and a phone field with US flag. Primary action: Invite Member."
      caption="Adding family members ships in V1. Create-and-assign digital cards live inside Accounts, no separate research step on remittance rates. The act of inviting is the act of moving money."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 50, label: "usability test participants", suffix: "+", color: "white" },
        { n: 15, label: "drop in primary-flow bounce", suffix: "%", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 700, label: "followers pre-A/B-tested launch", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 2100, label: "followers at App Store release", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The 50-participant usability round validated the primary flows; A/B-tested launch messaging grew the channel by 125% before the App Store hit. The Figma primitives we built around the multi-product structure carried the next partner-product addition into design with no redesign.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · WHAT GENERALIZES ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What generalizes" deck="Two things I'd carry into any product serving a multi-context audience.">
    <ul class="generalize">
      <li>
        <strong>The thesis is the tiebreaker.</strong>
        When two equally defensible options run even on the matrix, the question becomes "which one reads more like the product we said we were going to build." That's not a UX heuristic. It's the only honest answer when the UX answer is "both."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bilingual is worth a second pass.</strong>
        Treating Spanish as a translation layer ships a product that works in two languages but feels designed in one. Treating bilingual as a core requirement shapes every screen's information hierarchy. That principle generalizes to any product whose audience moves between contexts.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p class="muted">Credit to my Crediverso collaborators on design, product, engineering, and marketing — and to the family of avatars (Juan, Mateo, Maria) who anchored every sprint.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.48); font-size: 14.5px; }

  .cv-findings {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .cv-findings li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 92px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
  }
  .cv-pct {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 28px;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .cv-findings li span:last-child {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78);
  }
  .cv-findings strong { color: var(--cs-text); font-weight: 700; }

  .cv-goals {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 14px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .cv-goals { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .cv-goals li {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
    display: grid;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .cv-goals-num {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .cv-goals h4 {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--cs-text);
    margin: 0 0 4px;
  }
  .cv-goals p {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.72);
    margin: 0 !important;
    max-width: none !important;
  }

  .generalize {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .generalize li {
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
  }
  .generalize li strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
